REPUBLIC OF CAMEROON   Peace   –   Work   –   Fatherland  NORTH WEST REGIONAL ASSEMBLY  Assemblée Régionale du Nord Ouest · Special Status Region  March 2026 Session · 2025 Accounts   NWRA · Documentation Production Cell   Page 32  directed toward 100,000 FCFA per day, with all revenue paid into the Assembly account and use restricted (not to political organisations or confessional structures, per Article 406).  “I don’t think that by giving 200,000 francs here and we get five to ten  million [in goodwill], we are doing ourselves good.”   —   President, House of  Divisional Representatives  The New Anti-Corruption Unit  The Questor read the CONAC framework (Presidential Decree No. 2006/088 of 13 March 2006), which requires public and quasi-public institutions to set up their own units.  RESOLUTION D3-C   –   ANTI-CORRUPTION UNIT   The REC is authorised to establish and appoint an Anti-Corruption Unit of eight members on an equal basis   —   four from the Assembly and four from civil society (one Chairperson, six Members, one Secretary)   —   mandated to promote ethics, receive and investigate denunciations, protect whistleblowers, and report to the REC with a copy to CONAC; funded from the Assembly budget. Agreed without objection  as “a legal provision and a moral duty.”  A reflection from Dr. Njingu Musa, Questor of the National Assembly, closed the  day’s contributions, pressing four themes: a project is a provision that must be  actively sought; criticise privately and build publicly; put the social dimension first; and convene a joint seminar with mayors and MPs to model cooperation.  7. Closing Sitting  The closing sitting (joint and plenary), chaired by the President of the REC, brought the March 2026 Session to a close after the House of Chiefs returned a

--- Page 33 ---
REPUBLIC OF CAMEROON   Peace   –   Work   –   Fatherland  NORTH WEST REGIONAL ASSEMBLY  Assemblée Régionale du Nord Ouest · Special Status Region  March 2026 Session · 2025 Accounts   NWRA · Documentation Production Cell   Page 33  positive opinion on the deliberations. It featured two technical capacity presentations (the Bamenda – Bafut – Wom road ESIA and Disaster Risk Management), a lecture on the Special Status, the final adoption of the eight deliberations, a cultural address, and statements by the Representative of State and the President. The sitting began roughly two hours behind schedule, prompting a renewed appeal on punctuality.  7.1 ESIA   —   Bamenda – Bafut – Wom Road  Presented by Mr. Alfred Agia (environmental expert) with Mr. Sylvester Toh (civil society / follow-up committee). The project was handed over by the Minister of Public Works to a joint venture (AIC of Italy, Intec of Yaoundé, and Apsu Consultancy) and is to be funded by the African Development Bank (AfDB). The road runs from Bamenda (hospital roundabout) through Mbakong and Befang to Wom, over hilly, fertile terrain crossed by the Mezam and Menchum rivers, with the Mancon – Bafut section in very poor condition.  KEY ENVIRONMENTAL RISK   –   LAKE ILUM   The experts recommended re-routing the road away from the climb past Lake Ilum on Befang Hill: deep cutting could breach the lake bed, causing downstream flooding and loss of life, while loss of the lake would remove its local climate-regulating effect over Wom. The project owner is now working on a diversion at that point.  Socio-economic benefits include employment, lower transport fares, easier movement of produce (including toward Nigeria), tourism, and improved access to health and education; negative impacts include displacement and demolition, risk to sacred sites and graveyards, and public-health risks. Physical impacts include dust and emissions, spills, waste and habitat loss, with reforestation recommended. Risk classification: accidental spills low; injuries and snakebites medium; erosion high.

--- Page 34 ---
REPUBLIC OF CAMEROON   Peace   –   Work   –   Fatherland  NORTH WEST REGIONAL ASSEMBLY  Assemblée Régionale du Nord Ouest · Special Status Region  March 2026 Session · 2025 Accounts   NWRA · Documentation Production Cell   Page 34  Compensation scope   Expanded right-of-way   Maintained road  Houses / buildings   12 billion FCFA   ≈5 billion FCFA  Crops   46 million FCFA   18 million FCFA  Land with titles   1.2 billion FCFA   (included)  Total provision   ≈13.4 billion FCFA   ≈5.6 billion FCFA  Residents were advised to secure land titles to ease compensation, while noting that acquiring land in bad faith after gaining knowledge of the project would be improper and possibly unlawful.  7.2 Disaster Risk Management and Climate Resilience  Honourable Fuchi, environment focal point, presented on mainstreaming Disaster  Risk Management (DRM) into development projects, illustrating the Region’s  multi-hazard environment (the Bamenda – Fundong landslide threat, the Fundong market disaster, and the 1986 Lake Nyos gas disaster). The institutional framework runs from the Presidency and the National Council for Civil Protection through MINAT and sector ministries to decentralised actors, governed by Law No. 86/016 of 6 December 1986.  RECOMMENDATIONS   –   DISASTER RISK MANAGEMENT   Make DRM compulsory in all regional investment projects; strengthen ESIAs to cover disaster and climate risk; fund prevention, preparedness, early warning and recovery; support community disaster-preparedness committees; create a regional ad-hoc DRM committee; and support hazard mapping. Core message:  “look before you leap.”

--- Page 35 ---
REPUBLIC OF CAMEROON   Peace   –   Work   –   Fatherland  NORTH WEST REGIONAL ASSEMBLY  Assemblée Régionale du Nord Ouest · Special Status Region  March 2026 Session · 2025 Accounts   NWRA · Documentation Production Cell   Page 35  7.3 Highlights on the Special Status  Mr. Ernest Cho Ngu (Director of Human Resources for Councils and Regional  Assemblies, MINDEVEL) explained the Assembly’s organisation: 90 members (70  divisional representatives, 20 from the traditional command); a bicameral structure of the House of Divisional Representatives (five committees) and the House of Chiefs (20 members, two committees, headed by a Fon who is statutory Vice- President). Under Article 62 of the Constitution and Article 327 of the Code, the North West and South West regions enjoy a specific regime   —   participating in national   policy   on   the   Anglophone   education   subsystem,   managing   regional development authorities, contributing to the status of traditional chiefs, and being consulted on common-law and State public-service matters   —   with due respect for  the unitary State. Adopted deliberations move by a “shuttle” to the House of Chiefs  within 24 hours, then to the Representative of State for approval.  Councillors’ rights (participation, session allowances, training, health, protection,  transport and funeral assistance   —   with pension and remuneration for Bureau members only) and obligations (service, residency for Bureau members, abnegation, confidentiality) were set out, with clarifications that members resident at the seat are not entitled to transport/lodging allowances and that pension rights accrue at the end of the mandate.  7.4 Members’ Interventions  Members raised: caution against securing land titles in bad faith and a call to  publicise the road project via the Assembly website and WhatsApp; a geologist’s  recommendation for borehole drilling / coring on the proposed Lake Ilum deviation given the friable pyroclastic material on the Cameroon Volcanic Line; the allowance  and pension clarifications; a Santa councillor’s report of disrespect at a municipal reception, prompting calls for mayors to respect councillors’ advisory status; a report

